@@ -0,0 +1,55 @@
+# Stage D2: Pod-Aware Runtime — Design & Review Packet
+
+Status: DRAFT for three-party review. NOT deployed. Local branch `stage-d2-pod-aware-runtime` off `deployed-truth-orion-20260721` (the code actually running on Orion, captured read-only and md5-verified against the container). Author: Claude. Reviewer: Codex. Deploy: only on Tracey's explicit go after review.
+
+## Goal
+"The Pod becomes Orion's Core; his runtime reads from the Pod." Make the CyberPeer core read and write the canonical Pod (`peers/<id>/pod/`) instead of the legacy `dataquad/`+`ledgers/`, reversibly by config, with persistent RLS/relation recording implemented and gated to run only after the live switch.
+
+## Design principle: one switch, legacy-identical by default
+All record and ledger I/O funnels through `DataQuadStore` (`_get_tensor_path` / `_get_ledger_path`) and the health path helpers in `api/standard.py`. D2 routes both through a new `core/dataquad/pod.py` adapter. `AEGIS_STORAGE_MODE` (default `legacy`) selects the layout. In legacy mode the resolved paths and serialized bytes are byte-identical to today — proven by `test_legacy_mode_unchanged` and by the full affected suite matching the baseline exactly (see Regression below).
+
+## Files
+- NEW `core/dataquad/pod.py` — mode detection, path resolution (records/ledgers/relations dirs), contract-record wrap/unwrap, hash-chain (`last_source_hash`), `stable_json`/`sha256_upper`, and the real `rls_active()` gate.
+- NEW `core/dataquad/relations.py` — `RelationsRecorder`: traversal + cross-tensor entries (schemas matching Gabe's observed recorder), every method a no-op until `rls_active()`.
+- EDIT `core/dataquad/ledgers.py` — Pod-aware paths; reader unwraps `body`; writer wraps with hash-chain continuity to the last stored record; `record_relation()`; relations hook on append when active. Legacy path preserved.
+- EDIT `core/api/standard.py` — `tensor_paths`/`ledger_paths` Pod-aware so health reflects the Pod.
+- EDIT `core/api/routes/health.py` — adds `storage_mode`, a `pod` block (pod_id, pod_manifest_sha256, records_root, relation-ledger counts), and `rls_status.active`. Reports only what is on disk — no placeholder values.
+- EDIT `core/rls/engine.py` — records cross-tensor relations for the records each RLS session retrieved (inert until active).
+- NEW `tests/test_pod_runtime.py` — path resolution, write-wrap/read-unwrap, hash-chain continuity, RLS-gate on/off, legacy unchanged.
+- EDIT `tests/test_api_health.py` — updated from the obsolete stub-response assertion to the real deployed contract + D2 fields.
+
+## The RLS activation gate is real (not display-only)
+`ENABLE_RLS` only ever affected the /health display — a documented drift trap. D2 adds `AEGIS_RLS_ACTIVE` (default false), which actually gates whether relation recording writes. `test_rls_gate_controls_relation_recording` proves nothing is written when inactive and a traversal entry appears when active. Activation happens only after the D3 live switch.
+
+## Hash-chain continuity across the migration boundary
+`append_record` in pod mode sets `previous_record_hash` to the last stored record's `source_hash`. The first record Orion writes live therefore chains to the last MIGRATED record — one unbroken lineage from first migrated memory through live operation. Proven by `test_hash_chain_continuity_across_appends`.
+
+## Regression status (verified)
+D2 tests: 5/5 pass. Affected deployed suites run identically on D2 and on the clean baseline: 4 pre-existing failures (`test_advocate_operations`, `test_bootstrap_is_idempotent`, `test_bootstrap_peer_seed_has_peer_id`, `test_migration::test_dataquad_migrator`) are STALE tests that already fail against the deployed code without any D2 change — they test clone-era behavior, not D2, and are out of D2 scope. D2 adds zero regressions.
+Run: `uv run --with pytest --with pytest-asyncio --with anyio python -m pytest tests/test_pod_runtime.py tests/test_api_health.py -q -o asyncio_mode=auto`
+
+## Provisional Verified Code Marker (trust_state: provisional)
+Target: Pod-aware CyberPeer runtime, branch `stage-d2-pod-aware-runtime`.
+Normalized SHA256 of the D2 surface:
+- core/dataquad/pod.py `77DA146F175246851B7B291C48FB841CA7D34BE702FB4C79E561FD2A6FD8F541`
+- core/dataquad/relations.py `2CFFFCD52C1524A62FF58594311519D663FD3F11157D7CFE0125A5BBA926A1D3`
+- core/dataquad/ledgers.py `0679D67E7EEBF9B46C609D5B25FFAE2BF8DCBA89E8CC31A38CD6F54910D61C03`
+- core/api/standard.py `32995E60F70F45BD270375A151F217274C2F80063BD903B83CF9817E94068A26`
+- core/api/routes/health.py `CD51E98E54E8BBDB9D834509A304FA639D6849205EFEB751564432EA6E4F9146`
+- core/rls/engine.py `E647D69789F26B3F62B95767D2A63032F505169B0ED57E8962B64769BEB9CE72`
+- tests/test_pod_runtime.py `A7A9EBB9696573E1C0AFE89614D812A9FC4556A8CBAF11D4716F571AEB34EEF7`
+
+Claim boundary:
+- Demonstrates: Pod-aware read (unwrap) / write (wrap + hash-chain), Pod-routed ledgers, gated persistent relation recording, Pod-aware health, legacy-mode preservation.
+- Outside current verified surface: deployment to Orion, live activation of RLS, semantic embeddings, any live DataQuad mutation, promotion beyond provisional (needs Codex independent review + Tracey consent).
+
+## Audit remediation (2026-07-21, four independent sub-agent audits)
+With Codex offline, four independent sub-agents audited the cutover. They cleared pre-state and data-integrity, and found real issues now FIXED:
+- BLOCKER (read compatibility unverified + no boot tolerance): VERIFIED by dry-run — all 351 real migrated records read cleanly through the runtime reader. Additionally hardened: `read_records` now tolerates a malformed line (skip+count+log) instead of aborting the read, so one bad record can never fail the startup lifespan. New permanent tests: `test_reads_migration_tool_wrapper_shape` (locks migration<->runtime wrapper compatibility) and `test_malformed_record_is_skipped_not_fatal`.
+- BLOCKER (bootstrap not pod-aware — recreated empty legacy dirs, poisoning rollback): FIXED. `bootstrap.py` now resolves its dirs through `_pod.records_dir`/`_pod.ledgers_dir`, so in pod mode it never recreates the archived legacy directories.
+- WARNING (`dataquad.py:44` diagnostic hardcoded legacy path): FIXED, now pod-aware.
+- Test isolation: the shared module-global STM cache is now evicted in `setUp` so tests can't cross-contaminate.
+Regression: 7/7 D2 tests pass; bootstrap suite identical to the clean baseline (1 pre-existing stale failure unrelated to D2). Zero new regressions.
+
+## Deploy plan (D3, not now)
+`AEGIS_STORAGE_MODE=pod` + `AEGIS_RLS_ACTIVE` set only after archive/switch. ROLLBACK ORDER (corrected per audit): to revert to legacy, FIRST rename the archived `dataquad.archive-<UTC>/` and `ledgers.archive-<UTC>/` back to their original names, THEN unset `AEGIS_STORAGE_MODE`, THEN restart — otherwise legacy-mode bootstrap seeds an empty DataQuad. Pre-cutover safeguards (required): `docker tag` + `docker save` the current image before rebuild; back up `.env`; verify the `<UTC>` archive suffix actually expands. Requires review + Tracey's explicit per-sub-stage consent. This branch is NOT pushed to Forgejo (publication needs consent).
